      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "It would leave several consequential points open rather than assume them:\n\n- **Aim and threshold:** what counts as success\u2014profit, cash generation, market presence, or something else\u2014and by when.\n- **The alternatives:** open next spring versus waiting, choosing another Bristol format/location, or investing in the first caf\u00e9 instead.\n- **Demand:** expected local footfall, customer mix, repeat visits, and how much trade would be diverted from the existing caf\u00e9.\n- **Economics:** rent, fit-out, staffing, rates, utilities, stock, pricing, gross margin, and the cash buffer required before opening.\n- **Capacity and timing:** whether management and hiring capacity are available for a spring opening, and when the caf\u00e9 would reach a stable trading level.\n- **Risks:** construction or licensing delays, lease terms, a weaker-than-expected site, staffing shortages, and pressure on the first caf\u00e9\u2019s performance.\n- **Constraints:** maximum investment, acceptable loss period, and any non-financial limits such as your time or operational quality.\n\nThe most important missing framing is the success test: without it, \u201cshould we open?\u201d cannot distinguish a promising growth move from an unacceptable cash or management risk."
